<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paul the Apostle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 5 – c. 64/65 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Saul of Tarsus, Saint Paul</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paul, born Saul of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a>, was a Christian apostle who spread the teachings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> in the first century CE and founded several Christian communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> from the mid-40s to the mid-50s. His life is known chiefly from his letters and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-the-apostles">Acts of the Apostles</a>. According to Acts, he was a Pharisee who persecuted the followers of Jesus until a vision of Christ on the road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> led him to be baptised. Fourteen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> books are traditionally attributed to him, of which scholars regard seven as undisputed. His death in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> is traditional, dated either after the fire of 64 or a few years later.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paul-the-apostle.jpg" alt="Paul the Apostle" width="330" height="391" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fresco_of_Saint_Paul_at_Ephesus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Paul of Tarsus</b>, a Jewish convert who had at first persecuted the believers, became the great missionary to the Gentiles.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Thirteen letters present <b>Paul the Apostle</b> as their author: nine to churches and four to individuals.</blockquote><a href="/work/new-testament">New Testament · What It Is</a></li></ul></section>
